# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Sections III and IV of v23 as a block list.
P text: {n;m} citation numbers as in the document, (EQ:key) numbered equation reference, $...$ inline math (OMML).
EQ: list of (key, latex) or (key, [latex line 1, latex line 2]) for a two-line display under one number.
FIG: (n, new caption text after 'Fig. n.'). ALG: algorithm box."""

BLOCKS = [
("H1", "III. System Architecture"),
("H2", "A. Two Cores and the Interface"),
("P", "Fig. 1 presents the architecture. Two cores meet at two interfaces. The simulation core owns the hazard state $S_k$ and advances it with the transition $\\Phi$. The decision core reads an observation $O_k$ of that state and writes the intervention field $U_{DSS,k}$. An operator edits the inputs through the dashboard operator $\\Theta_{UI}$ and never the state. The external sources form the two sets of (EQ:fin). The first drives the fire dynamics, the second guides decisions only. One step of the loop is (EQ:loop)."),
("EQ", [("fin", [r"\mathcal{F}_{in,k}=\{U_{Meteo,k}(x,y),\,U_{Geo}(x,y),\,U_{Fuel,k}(x,y),\,U_{Ign,k}(x,y),\,U_{DSS,k}(x,y)\}", r"\mathcal{F}_{DSS,k}=\{U_{Val,k}(x,y),\,U_{Res,k}(x,y)\}"]),
        ("loop", [r"S_k(x,y) \to O_k(x,y)=h\big(S_k(x,y),\epsilon_k(x,y)\big) \to U_{DSS,k}(x,y)=\pi_{DSS}\big(O_k(x,y),\mathcal{F}_{DSS,k}\big)", r"\to U_{DSS,k}\subset \mathcal{F}_{in,k} \to S_{k+1}(x,y)=\Phi\big(S_k(x,y),\mathcal{F}_{in,k}\big)"])]),
("P", "In (EQ:fin), $k$ indexes the simulation step at $t_k = k\\,\\Delta t_{sim}$ and $(x,y)$ a cell of the grid $\\mathcal{G}$. The meteorology field $U_{Meteo,k}(x,y)$ holds wind speed $W_{ws,k}$, wind direction $W_{wd,k}$, air temperature, relative humidity, and precipitation, from stations and forecasts. The topography field $U_{Geo}(x,y)$ holds elevation, slope $G_{slope}$, aspect $G_{aspect}$, and road accessibility $G_{access}$, and it does not change in time. The fuel field $U_{Fuel,k}(x,y)$ holds the fuel type $F_{type}$ of a standard fuel model, the fuel load, and the dead fuel moisture $F_{moist,k}$, which weather updates. The ignition field $U_{Ign,k}(x,y)\\in\\{0,1\\}$ injects detected hot spots and scenario ignitions. The intervention field $U_{DSS,k}(x,y)$ is the only component the decision core writes. The value field $U_{Val,k}(x,y)$ aggregates buildings, critical facilities, population, and evacuation access into a value at risk per cell. The resource field $U_{Res,k}(x,y)$ holds crews and their capacity, availability, position, travel time, and suppression sources such as water. The two decisional fields never enter $\\Phi$."),
("P", "In (EQ:loop), $h$ is the observation function with the epistemic uncertainty $\\epsilon_k$, $\\pi_{DSS}$ the decision map of Section IV, and $\\Phi$ the transition of (EQ:burn) to (EQ:tau). Only $\\Phi$ writes the state. The simulation core is replaceable. Any hazard model that exposes its state through $h$ and accepts interventions through $\\mathcal{F}_{in,k}$ can take its place, since the decision core never reads the state directly. A purpose-built core is used for one reason. It is seeded, snapshotted, and replayed exactly, so the same incident can be run with and without a decision mechanism, and the difference is attributed to the mechanism alone."),
("H2", "B. Grid State and Transition"),
("P", "The state is the collection of per-cell states (EQ:state). The transition advances the four components in one step, as in (EQ:burn) to (EQ:tau). The burning status (EQ:burn) is the maximum of four mechanisms. Persistence keeps a burning cell alight while fuel remains above the extinction load and the fuel moisture stays below the moisture of extinction of its fuel type {6}. Propagation ignites a cell when the influence of its burning neighbors (EQ:psi) exceeds a calibrated threshold, the cellular rule of spread simulators such as {23}. Spotting ignites a downwind cell on a seeded ember. Injection ignites on $U_{Ign,k}$. Fuel (EQ:fuel) decreases through combustion in burning cells and through suppression, never below zero. The intensity proxy (EQ:inten) is a bounded function of fuel, wind, and slope, the three drivers of fireline intensity in the Rothermel model {6}. The ignition clock (EQ:tau) counts active burning and resets on extinction."),
("EQ", [("state", r"S_k=\{s_k(x,y)\}_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{G}},\qquad s_k(x,y)=\big(B_k(x,y),\,F_{load,k}(x,y),\,I_k(x,y),\,\tau_k(x,y)\big)"),
        ("burn", [r"B_{k+1}(x,y)=\max\{B^{pers}_{k+1},\,B^{prop}_{k+1},\,B^{spot}_{k+1},\,U_{Ign,k}\}(x,y)", r"B^{pers}_{k+1}=B_k\,\mathbf{1}[F_{load,k}>\epsilon_F]\,\mathbf{1}[F_{moist,k}<m_{ext}(F_{type})],\qquad B^{prop}_{k+1}=\mathbf{1}[\Psi_k>\Theta_{ign}]"]),
        ("fuel", r"F_{load,k+1}(x,y)=\max\{0,\;F_{load,k}-B_k\,F_{load,k}\,F_{burn,k}-F_{red,k}\}(x,y)"),
        ("inten", r"I_{k+1}(x,y)=B_{k+1}(x,y)\,f_{int}\big(F_{load,k},\,W_{ws,k},\,G_{slope}\big)(x,y)"),
        ("tau", r"\tau_{k+1}(x,y)=\begin{cases}0 & B_{k+1}=1\wedge B_k=0\\ \tau_k(x,y)+\Delta t_{sim} & B_{k+1}=1\wedge B_k=1\\ 0 & B_{k+1}=0\end{cases}")]),
("P", "Here $B_k\\in\\{0,1\\}$ is the burning status, $F_{load,k}\\in[0,1]$ the available fuel mass, $I_k$ the intensity proxy, and $\\tau_k$ the time since ignition. $\\epsilon_F=0.03$ is the extinction load, $m_{ext}(F_{type})$ the moisture of extinction of the fuel type, $\\Psi_k$ the propagation influence of (EQ:psi), $\\Theta_{ign}=0.125$ the ignition threshold relative to the base rate, $F_{burn,k}$ the combustion fraction per step, $F_{red,k}$ the suppression term of (EQ:supp), and $f_{int}$ a bounded intensity map."),
("H2", "C. Spread Model"),
("P", "Propagation is anisotropic. Each burning neighbor contributes its rate of spread weighted by the cosine of the angle between the wind and the direction to the target cell, as in (EQ:psi). The rate is a Rothermel-type function of wind, slope, aspect, moisture, and fuel type {6}, as in (EQ:ros). The implementation adds a wind-elongated elliptical correction and advances the front in substeps, so spread never exceeds one cell per substep. Ember spotting is the only stochastic element. A burning cell above an intensity threshold under dry wind releases an ember with a seeded probability, and the ember ignites a downwind cell if fuel remains there. Precipitation raises $F_{moist,k}$ toward the extinction level with a time constant of about thirty minutes, so a front dies out gradually."),
("EQ", [("psi", [r"\Psi_k(x,y)=\frac{1}{8}\sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{N}_8(x,y)} B_k(i,j)\,R_k(i,j)\,g_k^{(i,j)\to(x,y)}", r"g_k^{(i,j)\to(x,y)}=\max\{0,\cos(W_{wd,k}(i,j)-\varphi_{(i,j)\to(x,y)})\}"]),
        ("ros", r"R_k(x,y)=f_{ros}\big(W_{ws,k},\,W_{wd,k},\,G_{slope},\,G_{aspect},\,F_{moist,k},\,F_{type}\big)(x,y)")]),
("P", "In (EQ:psi), $\\mathcal{N}_8(x,y)$ is the eight-connected neighborhood, $R_k$ the rate of spread, $g_k$ the directional weight, and $\\varphi$ the direction from the neighbor to the target. In (EQ:ros), $f_{ros}$ is the Rothermel-type map of the surface fire spread model {6}, with the fuel parameters of the standard fuel models and the wind and slope factors of that model."),
("H2", "D. Suppression"),
("P", "Suppression never writes the burning state. It acts on fuel through the reduction term of (EQ:supp), which converts fielded capacity into a normalized fuel reduction gated by availability, effectiveness, travel time, and access. Hence, an order changes what the fire can burn, and the transition decides what burns."),
("EQ", [("supp", r"F_{red,k}(x,y)=f_{supp}\big(R_{cap,k},\,R_{avail,k},\,R_{eff,k},\,R_{time,k},\,G_{access}\big)(x,y)")]),
("P", "Here $R_{cap,k}$ is the capacity that $U_{DSS,k}$ allocates to the cell, $R_{avail,k}$ the availability from $U_{Res,k}$, $R_{eff,k}$ the effectiveness at the cell, which falls with the local intensity $I_k$, $R_{time,k}$ the travel time, and $G_{access}$ the accessibility from $U_{Geo}$."),
("H2", "E. Observation, Clocks, and Replay"),
("P", "The observation function projects the state onto the cells and injects epistemic uncertainty at this point only, as in (EQ:obs). The error is bounded by the complement of the cell confidence $\\gamma_k$ of (EQ:conf). Hence, a fully confident cell is observed exactly, an unobserved cell admits the worst case $\\epsilon_{max}$, and no distribution is assumed. The two cores run on different clocks. The simulation core advances at every step $\\Delta t_{sim}$. The decision core is invoked once per decision cycle of several steps, and its field is held constant in between. A snapshot holds the state, the moisture field, the cost integrals, and the random state. Two runs from one snapshot differ only in the orders under test. This is the basis of every paired comparison in Section V."),
("EQ", [("obs", r"O_k(x,y)=h\big(S_k(x,y),\,\epsilon_k(x,y)\big),\qquad |\epsilon_k(x,y)|\le\big(1-\gamma_k(x,y)\big)\,\epsilon_{max}")]),

("H1", "IV. The Decision Core"),
("FIG", 2, "The decision core of one agent. Features (EQ:feat), concepts (EQ:sets) to (EQ:gate), closed layer (EQ:rule) to (EQ:keep), open layer (EQ:bandit) to (EQ:g78), coordinator (EQ:share) and (EQ:merge)."),
("P", "Fig. 2 presents one local agent. The grid is partitioned into $N$ regions $\\mathcal{G}_i$. Agent $i$ reads the region-restricted observation $O_k^i$, the restriction of $O_k$ in (EQ:obs) to $\\mathcal{G}_i$, runs the path from heterogeneous sources to an intervention candidate $U^i_{DSS,k}$, and the coordinator merges the $N$ candidates into $U_{DSS,k}$. Every step is a closed-form map or a gated test."),
("H2", "A. Sources, Features, and Confidence"),
("P", "The sources are weather stations and forecasts, satellite and airborne imagery, terrain and fuel maps, road networks, resource status, asset and population layers, and field reports. The agent reduces them to ten features in the unit interval (EQ:feat), namely fire intensity $z_1$, spread potential $z_2$, weather severity $z_3$, ignition proximity $z_4$, fuel load $z_5$, asset exposure $z_6$, resource accessibility $z_7$, access and road status $z_8$, suppression availability $z_9$, and temporal urgency $z_{10}$. Every cell carries a confidence composed from observability, coverage, freshness, and reliability by their minimum (EQ:conf). The state is under-observed rather than noisy {19}. Bayesian or Dempster-Shafer treatments presuppose distributional knowledge that real-time sensing lacks {20}."),
("EQ", [("feat", r"z^{i}_{f,k}=\xi_f\big(O_k^{i}\big)\in[0,1],\qquad f=1,\dots,10"),
        ("conf", r"\gamma_k(x,y)=\min\{\gamma_{obs},\,\gamma_{cov},\,\gamma_{fre},\,\gamma_{rel}\}(x,y)")]),
("P", "In (EQ:feat), $\\xi_f$ is the extraction map of feature $f$. In (EQ:conf), $\\gamma_{obs}$ is observability, $\\gamma_{cov}$ sensor coverage, $\\gamma_{fre}$ freshness, and $\\gamma_{rel}$ source reliability."),
("H2", "B. Concept Space"),
("P", "Each feature is fuzzified over a five-term partition of unity. The concept space composes the features into six base concepts, the base concepts into five decision concepts on two levels, and two of these into one coordination concept, with the sets of (EQ:sets). An activation is a normalized weighted sum of the memberships or activations below it (EQ:agg). Raw activations are never consumed. Each is gated by its confidence and blended with a persistence-carried prior (EQ:gate), so a lost observation decays geometrically and the activation snaps back when confidence returns. The five decision concepts, fire threat level $c^{(2)}_1$, asset exposure risk $c^{(2)}_2$, suppression feasibility $c^{(2)}_3$, intervention urgency $c^{(3)}_1$, and evacuation pressure $c^{(3)}_2$, are the only variables over which rules are written."),
("EQ", [("sets", [r"\mathcal{C}^{(1)}_1=\{z_1,z_2\},\ \mathcal{C}^{(1)}_2=\{z_2,z_3,z_4\},\ \mathcal{C}^{(1)}_3=\{z_3,z_5\},\ \mathcal{C}^{(1)}_4=\{z_6\},\ \mathcal{C}^{(1)}_5=\{z_7,z_8\},\ \mathcal{C}^{(1)}_6=\{z_9\}", r"\mathcal{C}^{(2)}_1=\{c^{(1)}_1,c^{(1)}_2,c^{(1)}_3\},\ \mathcal{C}^{(2)}_2=\{c^{(1)}_2,c^{(1)}_4\},\ \mathcal{C}^{(2)}_3=\{c^{(1)}_5,c^{(1)}_6\}", r"\mathcal{C}^{(3)}_1=\{c^{(2)}_1,c^{(2)}_2,z_{10}\},\ \mathcal{C}^{(3)}_2=\{c^{(2)}_2,1-z_8,z_{10}\},\ \mathcal{C}^{(4)}_1=\{c^{(2)}_1,c^{(2)}_2\}"]),
        ("agg", r"a^{(1)}_{n,k}=\sum_{f\in\mathcal{C}^{(1)}_n}\omega_{n,f}\,\mu_{A_{n,f}}(z_{f,k}),\qquad a^{(l)}_{n,k}=\sum_{m\in\mathcal{C}^{(l)}_n}\omega^{(l)}_{n,m}\,a^{(l-1)}_{m,k},\qquad \sum\omega=1"),
        ("gate", r"a^{eff}_{n,k}=\gamma_{n,k}\,a^{obs}_{n,k}+(1-\gamma_{n,k})\,\rho\,a^{eff}_{n,k-1},\qquad \gamma_{n,k}=\min_{f\in\mathcal{C}_n}\gamma_{f,k}")]),
("P", "In (EQ:sets), $\\mathcal{C}^{(l)}_n$ is the input set of concept $n$ at level $l$, and $c^{(1)}_1$ to $c^{(1)}_6$ are fire severity, spread hazard, fuel hazard, asset value, crew reachability, and logistics support; $c^{(4)}_1$ is the operational priority used only by the coordinator. In (EQ:agg), $a$ is an activation, $\\omega$ a nonnegative weight, and $\\mu_{A}$ a term membership. In (EQ:gate), $a^{obs}$ is the observed activation, $a^{eff}$ the gated one that rules consume, and $\\rho\\in[0,1)$ the persistence decay."),
("H2", "C. The Closed Decision Layer"),
("P", "Rules have the conjunctive TS form of (EQ:rule) with constant consequents over six intervention families, namely suppression effort, resource deployment, containment line, asset protection, evacuation, and public warning. Inaction is the zero point of every family. A rule fires with its weakest clause, and the candidate is the firing-weighted average of (EQ:cand), floored at one so a weakly supported conclusion gives a weak order. The base is sparse. It starts from five seed rules and must satisfy the coverage condition (EQ:cover) on every reachable reading."),
("EQ", [("rule", r"R_r:\ \text{IF } a^{eff}_{1,k} \text{ is } A_{r,1}\ \text{AND}\ \cdots\ \text{AND}\ a^{eff}_{5,k} \text{ is } A_{r,5}\ \text{THEN}\ u_k=v_r"),
        ("cand", r"u_{m,k}=\frac{\sum_r \mu_{r,k}\,v_{r,m}}{\max(1,\sum_r \mu_{r,k})},\qquad \mu_{r,k}=\min_{n\in ant(r)}\mu_{A_{r,n}}\big(a^{eff}_{n,k}\big)"),
        ("cover", r"\min_{a^{eff}\in[0,1]^5}\ \max_r\ \mu_r(a^{eff})\ \ge\ \alpha_{min}=0.05")]),
("P", "In (EQ:rule) to (EQ:cover), $R_r$ is rule $r$, $A_{r,n}$ the term it requires of concept $n$, $v_r$ its consequent vector, $u_{m,k}$ the ordered intensity of family $m$, $\\mu_{r,k}$ the firing strength, $ant(r)$ the set of concepts the rule tests, and $\\alpha_{min}$ the coverage floor."),
("P", "Every candidate is judged on a shadow copy of the simulation core over a horizon $H$ before anything reaches the field. The decision cost is the weighted sum of (EQ:cost) over the five terms of (EQ:terms), with weights 1, 1, 1, 0.2, 0.2. The first three form the physical cost $J_{phys}$ that every cross-configuration comparison reads, because a run that never intervenes carries no response cost. Acceptance is satisficing, as in (EQ:sat). The bound is the tighter of a fixed ceiling and a required gain over inaction. A quality test (EQ:qual) compares each scored concept with the strongest intensity the candidate assigns to the family that answers it. Below the threshold $\\eta$ the graduated fail-safe scales the offensive families by $\\lambda$ and never touches evacuation and warning."),
("EQ", [("cost", r"J=\frac{\sum_j w_j J_j}{\sum_j w_j},\quad j\in\{burn,asset,pop,resp,delay\},\qquad J_{phys}=\tfrac{1}{3}\big(J_{burn}+J_{asset}+J_{pop}\big)"),
        ("terms", [r"J_{burn}=\frac{x}{1+x},\ x=\frac{N_{burned}}{\rho_{burn}N_{burnable}},\qquad J_{asset}=\frac{V_{lost}}{V_{total}},\qquad J_{pop}=\min\Big\{1,\frac{P_{steps}}{P_{total}N_{dec}}\Big\}", r"J_{resp}=\min\Big\{1,\frac{C_{steps}}{C_{ref}N_{dec}}\Big\},\qquad J_{delay}=\min\Big\{1,\frac{\tau_{disp}}{\tau_{ref}}\Big\}"]),
        ("sat", r"\hat{J}_k(U_{DSS,k})\ \le\ \min\big\{J_{TH},\,(1-\delta_{gain})\,\hat{J}_k(0)\big\},\qquad \delta_{gain}=0.05"),
        ("qual", r"Q_k=1-\frac{1}{4}\sum_{n\in\mathcal{C}_Q}\Big|a^{eff}_{n,k}-\max_{f\in F(n)}u_{f,k}\Big|,\qquad \lambda_k=\min\{1,\,Q_k/\eta\}")]),
("P", "In (EQ:terms), $N_{burned}$ and $N_{burnable}$ count burned and burnable cells with $\\rho_{burn}=0.05$ the reference fraction, $V_{lost}$ and $V_{total}$ are lost and exposed asset value, $P_{steps}$ the person-steps inside burning cells, $P_{total}$ the population at risk, $N_{dec}$ the decision steps of the horizon, $C_{steps}$ the fielded capacity summed over steps, $C_{ref}$ the reference capacity, $\\tau_{disp}$ the capacity-weighted dispatch time, and $\\tau_{ref}$ a sixty-minute reference. In (EQ:sat), $\\hat{J}_k(\\cdot)$ is the forecast cost, $J_{TH}$ the ceiling, and $\\hat{J}_k(0)$ the no-action forecast. In (EQ:qual), $\\mathcal{C}_Q$ holds the four scored concepts, $F(n)$ the families that answer concept $n$, and $\\lambda_k$ the fail-safe factor."),
("P", "When the standing decision falls short, the layer adapts within its vocabulary, in the evolving fuzzy tradition {26}. The consequent step and the boundary shift of (EQ:step) act on rules already in the base. Rule instantiation writes a rule on the uncovered combination of the two most active concepts (EQ:inst), and term insertion refines an ambiguous reading (EQ:term). Every move is a trial on a working copy and is kept only when the forecast physical cost strictly falls (EQ:keep). Algorithm 1 gives one decision cycle."),
("EQ", [("step", [r"v_{r,m}\leftarrow \mathrm{clip}_{[0,1]}(v_{r,m}\pm\delta)", r"(a_A,b_A)\leftarrow(a_A-\delta,\,b_A-\delta),\qquad (c_{A^-},d_{A^-})\leftarrow(c_{A^-}-\delta,\,d_{A^-}-\delta)"]),
        ("inst", r"A_{new}=\big\{(c^{(1)},t^{*}_{c^{(1)}}),\,(c^{(2)},t^{*}_{c^{(2)}})\big\},\qquad t^{*}_{c}=\arg\max_{t\in T_c}\mu_t\big(a^{eff}_c\big)"),
        ("term", [r"\max_{t\in T_{c^{(1)}}}\mu_t\big(a^{eff}_{c^{(1)}}\big)<0.62\ \Rightarrow", r"T_{c^{(1)}}\leftarrow T_{c^{(1)}}\cup\mathrm{trap}(x-0.12,\,x-0.05,\,x+0.05,\,x+0.12),\qquad x=a^{eff}_{c^{(1)}}"]),
        ("keep", r"\text{kept}\iff\Delta\hat{J}_{phys}<0")]),
("P", "In (EQ:step), $\\delta=0.05$ is the step, and $(a_A,b_A,c_A,d_A)$ are the trapezoid corners of term $A$, whose rising edge coincides with the falling edge of its left neighbor $A^-$, so the partition of unity is preserved. In (EQ:inst), $c^{(1)}$ and $c^{(2)}$ are the two most active concepts, $T_c$ the term set of concept $c$, and $t^*_c$ its dominant term. In (EQ:term), $\\mathrm{trap}(\\cdot)$ is a narrow trapezoid centered on the crisp reading $x$."),
("ALG", ("Algorithm 1", "Closed layer, one decision cycle of agent $i$", [
    (0, "Input: observation $O_k^i$, rule base $R$, snapshot of the core, threshold $\\eta$"),
    (0, "features $z$ and confidences $\\gamma$ by (EQ:feat), (EQ:conf); activations $a^{eff}$ by (EQ:agg), (EQ:gate)"),
    (0, "fire $R$; candidate $u_k$ by (EQ:cand)"),
    (0, "shadow forecasts over $H$ from the snapshot: $\\hat{J}_k(u_k)$ and $\\hat{J}_k(0)$"),
    (0, "if $Q_k<\\eta$ by (EQ:qual) then scale the offensive families of $u_k$ by $\\lambda_k$"),
    (0, "if (EQ:sat) fails then"),
    (1, "for $t=1$ to $T_{max}$ do"),
    (2, "pick a move from (EQ:step), (EQ:inst), (EQ:term) on a working copy of $R$; forecast"),
    (2, "if (EQ:keep) then commit the move else roll back"),
    (1, "if a deficit remains then call Algorithm 2"),
    (0, "if $\\hat{J}_{phys}(u_k)>\\hat{J}_{phys}(0)$ then withhold the offensive families of $u_k$ (no-harm)"),
    (0, "Output: fielded candidate $u_k$, updated $R$"),
])),
("H2", "D. The Open Decision Layer"),
("P", "The open layer is entered when tuning and instantiation leave a deficit, when a coverage gap opens while fire is alive, or when spread renews under applied orders. A contextual bandit {32} chooses between another closed trial and a proposal, as in (EQ:bandit), with the forecast cost reduction as reward. The proposer is a pretrained LLM used in context only. It receives the gated activations, the firing rules, the deficit, the resource state, and the vocabulary of effects, sectors, and bands, and it returns one object. The object is a plain rule in the form of (EQ:rule), a macro that blends up to three families (EQ:macro), or a composed intervention of up to three clauses over seven verified effects and seven sectors (EQ:clause), as depicted in Fig. 3. The band geometry (EQ:geom) and the idempotent operators (EQ:oper) resolve a clause on the engine fields. Hence, the space is open at interventions and closed at effects."),
("EQ", [("bandit", [r"q_k=\arg\max_{q\in\mathcal{A}(e_k)}H(e_k,q)\ \text{with probability } 1-\epsilon_b", r"H(e_k,q_k)\leftarrow H(e_k,q_k)+\alpha_b\big(r_k-H(e_k,q_k)\big)"]),
        ("macro", r"\beta=\{(b_j,\beta_j)\}_{j\le 3},\qquad (\beta,v)\mapsto(b_j,\,\beta_j v)"),
        ("clause", [r"A=\{(e,s,[\rho_{in},\rho_{out}],\alpha)\}_{\le 3},\qquad e\in\{wet,clear,ignite,coat,draft,evacuate,prime\}", r"s\in\{head,flank,rear,ring,at\_fire,assets,populated\}"]),
        ("geom", r"B(C,\rho_{in},\rho_{out})=D_{\rho_{out}}(C)\setminus D_{\rho_{in}-1}(C),\qquad \kappa(x)=\frac{(x-c_F)\cdot w}{\|x-c_F\|}"),
        ("oper", [r"a=\mathrm{clip}_{[0,1]}(\alpha u),\qquad r^{M}_{ret}\leftarrow\max(r^{M}_{ret},a)", r"r^{M}_{cap}\leftarrow\min\big(r^{M}_{cap}\,(1+\beta_w(d_w)),\,1.5\,c_{max}\big)"])]),
("P", "In (EQ:bandit), $e_k$ is the context (deficit band and coverage flag), $q$ a stage, $\\mathcal{A}(e_k)$ the allowed stages, $H$ the value table, $r_k$ the reward, $\\alpha_b=0.05$ the step, and $\\epsilon_b=0.10$ the exploration rate. In (EQ:macro), $b_j$ is a family and $\\beta_j$ its weight. In (EQ:clause), $e$ is an effect, $s$ a sector, $[\\rho_{in},\\rho_{out}]$ a distance band in cells, and $\\alpha$ an amount. In (EQ:geom), $D_\\rho(C)$ is the set of cells within four-neighbor distance $\\rho$ of the core $C$ of the sector, $c_F$ the fire centroid, $w$ the mean wind, and $\\kappa$ the cosine that splits head, flank, and rear. In (EQ:oper), $u$ is the firing intensity of the rule, $r^M_{ret}$ and $r^M_{cap}$ the retardant and capacity fields on the mask $M$, $\\beta_w(d_w)$ the drafting gain at water distance $d_w$, and $c_{max}$ the capacity cap."),
("FIG", 3, "The effect grammar of (EQ:clause). A clause names an effect, a sector, a band, and an amount; (EQ:geom) and the masks resolve it to cells; (EQ:oper) writes the engine field."),
("P", "Every proposal is untrusted by construction and passes the gate chain of Fig. 4. G1 to G4 are algebraic and cost no simulation step. They check canonical form, vocabulary and duplication, relevance, which means that the rule fires on the current reading, and pool economy. G5 to G8 are simulation gates, as in (EQ:g56) and (EQ:g78). They demand a strict forecast gain on two independently seeded futures, a margin $\\delta$ for a package, and attribution, which means that the package beats its own rule stripped of the new object. A rejection returns the gate name and both forecasts, and the proposer may revise once. Algorithm 2 gives the loop."),
("EQ", [("g56", [r"\text{G5: }\hat{J}^{\xi_1}_{phys}(R\cup\{p\})<\hat{J}^{\xi_1}_{phys}(R)", r"\text{G6: }\hat{J}^{\xi_2}_{phys}(R\cup\{p\})<\hat{J}^{\xi_2}_{phys}(R)"]),
        ("g78", [r"\text{G7: }\hat{J}(R\cup\{p\})-\hat{J}(R)\le-\delta", r"\text{G8: }\hat{J}(R\cup\{r_A\})<\hat{J}(R\cup\{r\setminus A\})"])]),
("P", "In (EQ:g56) and (EQ:g78), $p$ is the proposal, $R$ the current base, $\\xi_1$ and $\\xi_2$ the two seeds, $r_A$ the rule carrying the new object $A$, and $r\\setminus A$ the same rule without it."),
("ALG", ("Algorithm 2", "Open layer, proposal and admission", [
    (0, "Input: situation summary $\\Sigma_k$ (activations, firing rules, deficit, resources, vocabulary), rule base $R$, seeds $\\xi_1$, $\\xi_2$"),
    (0, "$p \\leftarrow \\mathrm{LLM}(\\Sigma_k)$; $n \\leftarrow 0$"),
    (0, "repeat"),
    (1, "for $g$ in G1, G2, G3, G4 do if $p$ fails $g$ then go to step 8"),
    (1, "forecast $R$ and $R\\cup\\{p\\}$ on $\\xi_1$ and $\\xi_2$; test (EQ:g56)"),
    (1, "if $p$ is a package then test (EQ:g78)"),
    (1, "if all gates pass then $R\\leftarrow R\\cup\\{p\\}$; log origin and gate values; return admitted"),
    (1, "if $n=0$ then send the gate name and both forecasts to the LLM; $p\\leftarrow$ revision; $n\\leftarrow 1$ else return rejected"),
    (0, "until $n>1$"),
])),
("FIG", 4, "The gate chain of (EQ:g56) and (EQ:g78). Algebraic gates reject without a forecast; simulation gates compare the forecast with and without $p$ on two seeds."),
("H2", "E. Local Agents and Global Coordination"),
("P", "$N$ agents run Algorithms 1 and 2 over their own regions. The coordinator performs no inference. Each agent sends up its operational priority $p_i$, its resource request, and a confidence summary, and it receives the allocation, the priority weights, and the acceptance thresholds in force. Regions are ranked by priority and receive the attention share $\\sigma_i$ of (EQ:share), which scales only the offensive families. A monitored region also faces a stricter quality threshold. The merged field of (EQ:merge) is forecast by the no-harm guard against inaction. When the plan is worse, the offensive orders are withheld while the life-safety orders stand."),
("EQ", [("share", r"\sigma_i=\begin{cases}1 & p_i\ge\tau_{att}\,p_{max}\\ 0.5+0.5\,p_i/p_{max} & \text{otherwise}\end{cases},\qquad \tau_{att}=0.35"),
        ("merge", [r"U_{DSS,k}=\mathcal{A}_{DSS}\big(\{\sigma_i\,U^{i}_{DSS,k}\}_{i=1}^{N}\big)", r"\hat{J}_{phys}(U_{DSS,k})>\hat{J}_{phys}(0)\ \Rightarrow\ \text{offensive families withheld}"])]),
("P", "In (EQ:share), $p_i$ is the operational priority $c^{(4)}_1$ of region $i$, $p_{max}$ the highest priority, and $\\tau_{att}$ the attention threshold. In (EQ:merge), $\\mathcal{A}_{DSS}$ merges the scaled regional fields into one intervention field."),
("P", "The gates give the architecture four properties that hold whatever produced the candidate, because they are properties of the acceptance function and not of the proposer. Proposition 1 (no-harm). No order reaches the field whose forecast physical cost exceeds the no-action forecast over the guard horizon. Proposition 2 (life-safety invariance). Evacuation and warning intensities are never reduced by any gate, since neither the fail-safe nor the no-harm rule has the two families in its domain, and a proposal that lowers them fails G2. Proposition 3 (coverage). At every reachable reading either some rule fires above the coverage floor of (EQ:cover), or the layer falls back to the watchful posture and triggers adaptation. Proposition 4 (monotone descent). Along any sequence of admissions the forecast physical cost on the first seed decreases by at least $\\delta$ per admitted package, so the number of admissions in one incident is bounded and the extension halts once no proposal clears the gates. A fifth property is probabilistic. A proposal whose true effect is zero or harmful passes G5 and G6 with probability at most $q^2$, where $q$ is the probability that one shadow forecast reports a gain by chance. Section V-E reports how often admitted objects paid off in the realized outcome."),
]
